      <!-- Frequently Asked Questions -->
      <section>
        <div class="section-heading-wrap">
          <div class="section-tag">FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS</div>
          <h2 class="section-title">Common Developer Inquiries</h2>
          <p class="section-desc">Facts about RouteCase architecture, privacy, compatibility, and licensing.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-card" open>
            <summary class="faq-header">
              <span class="faq-q-text">What file extensions does RouteCase support?</span>
              <span class="faq-tag">Syntax &amp; Schema</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              RouteCase uses <code>.rcase</code> as its primary extension, and also fully recognizes <code>.routecase</code> and <code>*.rcase.json</code> files. When opened in VS Code, all files automatically activate the RouteCase language grammar, bundled JSON schema validation, hover tooltips, and real-time syntax checking.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-card">
            <summary class="faq-header">
              <span class="faq-q-text">Does RouteCase require an account, login, or internet telemetry?</span>
              <span class="faq-tag">Privacy &amp; Offline</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              <strong>Zero accounts, zero cloud dependencies, zero tracking.</strong> RouteCase is 100% offline and runs strictly on your local machine. Your API credentials, payloads, tokens, and test suites never leave your development environment. Sensitive headers like <code>Authorization</code>, <code>apiKey</code>, and session cookies are automatically masked with <code>[REDACTED]</code> in all log outputs.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-card">
            <summary class="faq-header">
              <span class="faq-q-text">Can I test local development servers without starting a proxy?</span>
              <span class="faq-tag">Local Testing</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              Yes! RouteCase connects directly to any reachable endpoint—including <code>http://localhost:3000</code>, <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080</code>, local Docker containers, private VPN environments, and public internet APIs. No separate background daemon or browser companion is required.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-card">
            <summary class="faq-header">
              <span class="faq-q-text">How does variable chaining and token capture work?</span>
              <span class="faq-tag">Workflow Automation</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              In any test case, provide a <code>capture</code> map to extract response JSON properties using dot notation (e.g. <code>"authToken": "data.accessToken"</code>). RouteCase persists these variables in memory during the execution run, enabling subsequent requests to reference them via template syntax: <code>"Authorization": "Bearer &#123;&#123;authToken&#125;&#125;"</code>.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-card">
            <summary class="faq-header">
              <span class="faq-q-text">Can I import existing OpenAPI 3.0 or Swagger 2.0 specifications?</span>
              <span class="faq-tag">Migration</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              Yes. RouteCase includes a native, zero-dependency OpenAPI importer. Press <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code> (or <code>Cmd+Shift+P</code> on macOS), run <strong><code>RouteCase: Import from OpenAPI / Swagger</code></strong>, select your JSON spec, and RouteCase instantly transforms your operations, paths, parameters, and schemas into executable <code>.rcase</code> suites.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-card">
            <summary class="faq-header">
              <span class="faq-q-text">How do I install RouteCase in Visual Studio Code?</span>
              <span class="faq-tag">Installation</span>
              <span class="faq-arrow"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-body">
              Open VS Code, press <code>Ctrl+Shift+X</code> (or <code>Cmd+Shift+X</code>), search for <strong><code>RouteCase</code></strong>, and click <strong>Install</strong>. Alternatively, install it directly from your command line with:
              <br /><br />
              <code>code --install-extension UtkarshKumar.routecase</code>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
